[FEATURE,API] Support to wrap Software in QVariant

These changes allow the Software metadata class to be converted to
QVariant, and saved using the QVariant API.

Specifically, the Q_DECLARE_METATYPE was used to wrap Software and the
two required stream operators (using QDataStream) were added.

Use of the QVariant is as follows:

  // obtain instance of Software and add to a QVariant
  // this should work without any additional code
  Software software = input[0].getDataProcessing()[0].getSoftware();
  QVariant softwareVariant;
  softwareVariant.setValue(software);

  // I think these two functions are both required for the
  // QVariant::save() and QVariant::load() methods. I know the second
  // *is* required because the stream operators need to be registered.
  qRegisterMetaType<Software>("Software");
  qRegisterMetaTypeStreamOperators<Software>("Software");

  // An example of the QVariant::save() method being called.
  QSettings settings;
  settings.setValue("Software", softwareVariant);
  settings.sync();

  // This should call the QVariant::load() method, and unpack it
  // into a Software instance.
  Software retrievedSoftware = settings.value("Software").value<Software>();

[FEATURE,API] Initial support to wrap DataProcessing in QVariant

These changes allow the DataProcessing metadata class to be converted to
QVariant, and saved using the QVariant API.

Specifically, the Q_DECLARE_METATYPE was used to wrap DataProcessing and the
two required stream operators (using QDataStream) were added.

Use of the QVariant is as follows:

  // obtain instance of DataProcessing and add to a QVariant
  // this should work without any additional code
  DataProcessing dp = input[0].getDataProcessing()[0];
  QVariant variant;
  variant.setValue(dp);

  // I think these two functions are both required for the
  // QVariant::save() and QVariant::load() methods. I know the second
  // *is* required because the stream operators need to be
  // registered.
  qRegisterMetaType<DataProcessing>("DataProcessing");
  qRegisterMetaTypeStreamOperators<DataProcessing>("DataProcessing");

  // An example of the QVariant::save() method being called.
  QSettings settings;
  settings.setValue("DataProcessing", variant);
  settings.sync();

  // This should call the QVariant::load() method, and unpack it
  // into a DataProcessing instance.
  DataProcessing retrivedDP = settings.value("DataProcessing").value<DataProcessing>();

[FEATURE,API] Support for wrapping SourceFile in QVariant

These changes allow the SourceFile metadata class to be converted to
QVariant, and saved using the QVariant API.

Specifically, the Q_DECLARE_METATYPE was used to wrap SourceFile and
the two required stream operators (using QDataStream) were added.

Use of the QVariant is as follows:

// obtain instance of SourceFile and add to a QVariant
// this should work without any additional code
SourceFile sf = input[0].getSourceFile();
QVariant variant;
variant.setValue(sf);

// I think these two functions are both required for the
// QVariant::save() and QVariant::load() methods. I know the
// second *is* required because the stream operators need to be
// registered.
qRegisterMetaType<SourceFile>("SourceFile");
qRegisterMetaTypeStreamOperators<SourceFile>("SourceFile");

// An example of the QVariant::save() method being called.
QSettings settings;
settings.setValue("SourceFile", variant);
settings.sync();

// This should call the QVariant::load() method, and unpack it
// into a SourceFile instance.
SourceFile retrivedSF =
  settings.value("SourceFile").value<SourceFile>();

